This is a "does this state model hold up" question, so I'd build one self-contained HTML file with free-play buttons and a few guided walkthroughs. I haven't written or run anything. Your facts didn't include the real state machine, so I've assumed one, and the assumptions are listed after the code.

## Where it lives

Put it next to the reservation module as `reservation-paid-after-cancelled.prototype.html`. Double-click to run, no build step, state in memory only.

## The model

- **States:** `pending`, `held`, `paid`, `cancelled`, `expired`
- **Separate flag:** `paymentInFlight`, because the likely bug is that payment confirmation is asynchronous and arrives after the cancel.
- **Events:** `reserve`, `startPayment`, `cancel`, `paymentSucceeded` (the webhook), `expire`
- **Mode toggle:** `naive` (transitions only check "is it already paid?") versus `guarded` (`paymentSucceeded` is rejected, and flagged for refund, when the status is `cancelled` or `expired`). Flipping it on the same walkthrough shows the fix working.
- **Detector:** the history is scanned after every step, and a red banner appears if `cancelled` is ever followed by `paid`.

## Walkthrough tabs

1. **Happy path:** reserve, startPayment, paymentSucceeded. Ends in `paid`.
2. **Cancel then click pay:** reserve, cancel, startPayment. The UI-level attempt should be rejected in both modes.
3. **The race (the real question):** reserve, startPayment, cancel, paymentSucceeded. Naive mode reaches `paid` after `cancelled`. Guarded mode stays `cancelled` and flags a refund.
4. **Expiry race:** reserve, startPayment, expire, paymentSucceeded. This is the same hole through a different door.
5. **Duplicate webhook:** paymentSucceeded twice after a cancel. This checks that the fix is idempotent.

## Sketch of the file

```html
<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>PROTOTYPE: paid-after-cancelled</title>
<body style="font-family:monospace;max-width:760px;margin:2em auto">
<h3>PROTOTYPE (throwaway): can a reservation reach paid after cancelled?</h3>
<label>Mode:
  <select id="mode"><option>naive</option><option>guarded</option></select>
</label>
<div id="tabs"></div>
<div id="free"></div>
<div id="banner"></div>
<pre id="state"></pre>
<pre id="log"></pre>

<script>
let s;
const reset = () => s = { status: "none", inFlight: false, needsRefund: false, history: [], log: [] };

const rules = {
  reserve:  s => s.status === "none" && (s.status = "held"),
  startPayment: s => s.status === "held" && (s.inFlight = true),
  cancel:   s => ["held","paid"].includes(s.status) && (s.status = "cancelled"),
  expire:   s => s.status === "held" && (s.status = "expired"),
  paymentSucceeded: s => {
    const dead = ["cancelled","expired"].includes(s.status);
    if (mode.value === "guarded" && dead) { s.inFlight = false; s.needsRefund = true; return true; }
    if (s.status !== "paid") { s.status = "paid"; s.inFlight = false; return true; }
  },
};

function fire(ev) {
  const before = s.status;
  const ok = rules[ev](s);
  s.log.push(`${ev}: ${ok ? "applied" : "REJECTED"}  (${before} -> ${s.status})`);
  s.history.push(s.status);
  render();
}

function render() {
  state.textContent = JSON.stringify({ status: s.status, inFlight: s.inFlight, needsRefund: s.needsRefund }, null, 2);
  log.textContent = s.log.join("\n");
  const h = s.history, i = h.indexOf("cancelled");
  const bad = i >= 0 && h.slice(i).includes("paid");
  banner.innerHTML = bad ? "<b style='color:red'>REACHED paid AFTER cancelled</b>" : "";
}

const walks = {
  "happy": ["reserve","startPayment","paymentSucceeded"],
  "cancel then pay": ["reserve","cancel","startPayment"],
  "race": ["reserve","startPayment","cancel","paymentSucceeded"],
  "expiry race": ["reserve","startPayment","expire","paymentSucceeded"],
  "duplicate webhook": ["reserve","startPayment","cancel","paymentSucceeded","paymentSucceeded"],
};
let walkStep = 0, walk = null;
tabs.innerHTML = Object.keys(walks).map(k => `<button onclick="pick('${k}')">${k}</button>`).join(" ")
  + ` <button onclick="stepWalk()">next step</button> <button onclick="pick(null)">reset</button>`;
free.innerHTML = Object.keys(rules).map(e => `<button onclick="fire('${e}')">${e}</button>`).join(" ");
function pick(k) { reset(); walk = k && walks[k]; walkStep = 0; render(); }
function stepWalk() { if (walk && walkStep < walk.length) fire(walk[walkStep++]); }
mode.onchange = () => pick(null);
reset(); render();
</script>
```

## What the result tells you

- If the **race** tab ends in `paid` in naive mode, the machine can reach paid after cancelled whenever payment confirmation is decoupled from the cancel. The actual fix is then to make `paymentSucceeded` check the current status, or to void or refund the in-flight payment on cancel.
- If the **cancel then pay** tab is rejected in both modes, the synchronous path is safe and the hole is only the async one.
- If the **duplicate webhook** tab flips anything in guarded mode, the fix isn't idempotent.

## Assumptions to check

- Payment confirmation really is asynchronous in your system. If it's synchronous and atomic with the status change, the race tab can't happen and the answer is likely "no".
- `cancel` is allowed from `paid` (as a refund path). If it isn't, drop that clause.
- There is a separate `expired` state. If expiry is just `cancelled`, tab 4 collapses into tab 3.

When you're done, fold the verdict into the real transition code, commit the HTML to a throwaway branch, and link that branch from the implementation issue along with the one-line answer.

